sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/metabolic-scaling-exponent--grok-4-7--1> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "PGLS of PanTHERIA basal rate against body mass" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/metabolic-scaling-exponent> ;
    dct:description "Use PanTHERIA 1.0, Wilson and Reeder 2005 file, columns 18-1_BasalMetRate_mLO2hr and the paired mass 5-2_BasalMetRateMass_g. The Ecological Archives metadata says metabolic rate was accepted only with a mass from the same individuals, measured resting, post-absorptive, in the thermoneutral zone. Drop the missing-value code -999. Primary regression is log10(BMR) on log10(paired mass), PGLS with Pagel's lambda, repeated on 100 trees from the Upham credible set. A species-typical mass sensitivity uses 5-1_AdultBodyMass_g and is reported separately. Model comparison, pre-registered: a single slope, order-specific slopes, and a quadratic term in log mass. The curved or order-specific model wins if its AIC beats the single power law by more than 10 on at least 95 of the 100 trees. The single-exponent claim is decided by the 95 percent interval of the single slope: it must exclude either 2/3 or 3/4. If the interval contains both, the question is underdetermined, which is the problem's own falsifier. Geographic mean temperature in PanTHERIA is not body temperature and is not used as a thermoregulatory control." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "On at least 95 of 100 trees, either a single-slope 95 percent interval that excludes 2/3 or excludes 3/4, or an order-specific or quadratic model that beats the power law by AIC of more than 10." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The single-slope interval contains both 2/3 and 3/4 and no alternative model beats the power law by the AIC gap. The universality question is then underdetermined on PanTHERIA." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/data-test" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Pre-register the columns, the missing code, the AIC gap and the two target exponents. 2. Drop -999 and match species to the Upham trees. 3. Fit Pagel's lambda PGLS on 100 trees for the paired-mass regression. 4. Compare single-slope, order-specific and quadratic models by AIC. 5. Repeat with adult body mass as a labeled sensitivity and do not pool the two masses." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Missing: A species-level body-temperature table joined to this taxonomy. PanTHERIA's geographic temperature is not a substitute, so the White and Seymour control is not replicated here." ;
    skos:note "Tool: phylogenetic-comparative-methods" ;
    skos:note "Tool: allometry" ;
    skos:note "Tool: data-test" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch02-01> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-10> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://esapubs.org/archive/ecol/E090/184/metadata.htm> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://vertlife.org/data/mammals/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1890/08-1494.1> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000494> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.276.5309.122> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0436428100> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.3733/hilg.v06n11p315> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/metabolic-scaling-exponent> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Is there a universal metabolic scaling exponent?" .

  <https://esapubs.org/archive/ecol/E090/184/metadata.htm> dct:title "PanTHERIA 1.0 Ecological Archives E090-184" ;
    dct:description "Basal metabolic rate in mL O2/hr and the paired body mass; missing code -999; Wilson and Reeder 2005 file has 5416 species records." .
  <https://vertlife.org/data/mammals/> dct:title "Upham mammal phylogenies" ;
    dct:description "One hundred trees for Pagel's lambda PGLS." .
  <https://doi.org/10.1890/08-1494.1> dct:title "PanTHERIA: a species‐level database of life history, ecology, and geography of extant and recently extinct mammals" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Jones et al. 2009, PanTHERIA" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000494> dct:title "Inferring the mammal tree: Species-level sets of phylogenies for questions in ecology, evolution, and conservation" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Upham, Esselstyn and Jetz 2019, mammal phylogenies" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.276.5309.122> dct:title "A General Model for the Origin of Allometric Scaling Laws in Biology" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "West, Brown and Enquist 1997, the 3/4 prediction" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0436428100> dct:title "Mammalian basal metabolic rate is proportional to body mass\n                    <sup>2/3</sup>" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "White and Seymour 2003, the 2/3 claim after physiological controls" .
  <https://doi.org/10.3733/hilg.v06n11p315> dct:title "Body size and metabolism" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Kleiber 1932, Body size and metabolism" .
}

